### What is graceful shutdown and why does it matter?

Graceful shutdown changes how the server stops. Without it, when the server receives a SIGTERM signal (the standard termination signal from the operating system or Kubernetes), it stops immediately and drops any requests that are still being processed. With graceful shutdown enabled, the server stops accepting new connections but continues processing in-flight requests for up to a configurable timeout.

This matters in production because Priya, the customer, might be in the middle of a fund transfer when a deployment happens. Without graceful shutdown, her transfer request could be dropped mid-way.

```yaml
# application.yml
server:
  shutdown: graceful

spring:
  lifecycle:
    timeout-per-shutdown-phase: 30s
```

```java
package com.bank.service;

import jakarta.annotation.PreDestroy;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TransferService.class);

    // This runs during graceful shutdown after in-flight requests complete
    @PreDestroy
    public void cleanup() {
        log.info("Closing database connections and flushing audit log");
    }
}
```

```text
Graceful shutdown timeline:
1. SIGTERM received
2. Server stops accepting NEW connections
3. In-flight requests continue processing (up to 30 seconds)
4. All in-flight requests complete
5. @PreDestroy callbacks execute (close connections, flush buffers)
6. ApplicationContext closes
7. JVM exits
```

### How does zero-downtime deployment work in Kubernetes?

In Kubernetes, zero-downtime deployment combines graceful shutdown with readiness probes and a preStop hook. The preStop hook adds a delay before SIGTERM so the pod is removed from the service endpoints first, preventing new requests from reaching a terminating pod.

```yaml
# Kubernetes deployment manifest
apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: national-bank-app
spec:
  replicas: 3
  strategy:
    type: RollingUpdate
    rollingUpdate:
      maxUnavailable: 0
      maxSurge: 1
  template:
    spec:
      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 60
      containers:
        - name: app
          image: national-bank-app:1.0.0
          lifecycle:
            preStop:
              exec:
                command: ["sh", "-c", "sleep 5"]
          readinessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /actuator/health/readiness
              port: 8080
            initialDelaySeconds: 10
            periodSeconds: 5
          livenessProbe:
            httpGet:
              path: /actuator/health/liveness
              port: 8080
            initialDelaySeconds: 15
            periodSeconds: 10
```

```text
Kubernetes rolling update flow:
1. New pod starts, passes readiness probe
2. Old pod receives preStop hook -> sleeps 5 seconds
3. K8s removes old pod from Service endpoints (no new traffic)
4. SIGTERM sent to old pod
5. Graceful shutdown: drain in-flight requests (30 seconds max)
6. Old pod exits cleanly
7. Zero requests dropped
```

### What are common mistakes with Spring Boot startup?

The most common mistake is putting heavy initialization logic in a bean constructor or `@PostConstruct` method, which blocks the entire startup and delays the application from becoming ready.

```java
// WRONG: heavy work in @PostConstruct blocks startup
package com.bank.service;

import jakarta.annotation.PostConstruct;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;

@Service
public class SlowStartupService {

    @PostConstruct
    public void init() {
        // This blocks startup for 30 seconds
        // All other beans wait, Tomcat does not start
        loadMillionRecordsFromDatabase();
    }
}

// CORRECT: use CommandLineRunner for heavy startup tasks
package com.bank.startup;

import org.springframework.boot.CommandLineRunner;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class DataLoaderRunner implements CommandLineRunner {

    private final DataLoadService dataLoadService;

    public DataLoaderRunner(DataLoadService dataLoadService) {
        this.dataLoadService = dataLoadService;
    }

    @Override
    public void run(String... args) {
        // Runs after Tomcat starts, does not block other beans
        dataLoadService.loadRecordsAsync();
    }
}
```

Another mistake is not configuring readiness probes. Without a readiness probe, Kubernetes sends traffic to a pod that is still starting up, causing 503 errors for Priya.

### What are the differences between liveness and readiness probes?

| Probe | Purpose | Failure Action | What to Include |
|---|---|---|---|
| Liveness | Is the JVM alive? | Kubernetes restarts the pod | Only internal JVM health |
| Readiness | Can the app serve traffic? | Kubernetes removes from load balancer | DB connectivity, cache, external services |

Pankaj designed the probes at National Bank of India with a critical rule: never include external dependencies in the liveness probe. If the database goes down, the liveness probe should still pass. A database outage should not cause pod restarts, which would make the situation worse. External dependencies belong only in the readiness probe.

```java
package com.bank.health;

import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.Health;
import org.springframework.boot.actuate.health.HealthIndicator;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;

@Component
public class CoreBankingHealthIndicator implements HealthIndicator {

    private final CoreBankingClient coreBankingClient;

    public CoreBankingHealthIndicator(CoreBankingClient coreBankingClient) {
        this.coreBankingClient = coreBankingClient;
    }

    @Override
    public Health health() {
        // This affects readiness, NOT liveness
        boolean isReachable = coreBankingClient.ping();
        if (isReachable) {
            return Health.up().withDetail("coreBanking", "reachable").build();
        }
        return Health.down().withDetail("coreBanking", "unreachable").build();
    }
}
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- The startup order matters for debugging `BeanCurrentlyInCreationException`. Circular dependencies (A depends on B, B depends on A) fail at startup. The fix is to redesign the dependency or use `@Lazy` on one injection point.
- `ApplicationReadyEvent` is the safest place to trigger post-startup logic because all beans are fully initialized and the server is accepting traffic.
- In a microservices architecture, Pankaj configures the `spring.main.banner-mode=off` in production to reduce log noise and sets `spring.jmx.enabled=false` to reduce memory overhead.
- The embedded server is not limited to Tomcat. You can switch to Jetty or Undertow by excluding `spring-boot-starter-tomcat` and adding the alternative starter.

```java
package com.bank.listener;

import org.springframework.boot.context.event.ApplicationReadyEvent;
import org.springframework.context.event.EventListener;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Component;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;

@Component
public class StartupNotifier {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(StartupNotifier.class);

    @EventListener(ApplicationReadyEvent.class)
    public void onReady() {
        log.info("National Bank of India application is fully started and ready");
    }
}
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- What is the difference between `CommandLineRunner` and `ApplicationRunner`? Both run after startup. `CommandLineRunner` receives raw `String[] args`. `ApplicationRunner` receives parsed `ApplicationArguments` with option support.
- Can you have multiple `CommandLineRunner` beans? Yes. Use `@Order` to control their execution sequence.
- What happens if a `CommandLineRunner` throws an exception? The application fails to start and exits. Wrap risky logic in try-catch if the task is non-critical.
- How do you change the embedded server from Tomcat to Jetty? Exclude `spring-boot-starter-tomcat` from `spring-boot-starter-web` and add `spring-boot-starter-jetty`.

```xml
<!-- pom.xml: switch from Tomcat to Jetty -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
    <exclusions>
        <exclusion>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-tomcat</artifactId>
        </exclusion>
    </exclusions>
</dependency>
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-jetty</artifactId>
</dependency>
```
